00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.710 align:start position:0%
 
it's no exaggeration to say that marvel  is one of the most prolific and  successful entertainment companies in  the world but it hasn't always been that  way and over the years the biggest name  in comics has produced some truly  spectacular duds these are marvel's  biggest failures  in 1978 marvel was riding high off the  success of its incredible hulk tv series  the time seemed right for a whole marvel  tv universe and in another world the  doctor strange pilot movie might well  have kicked it off watching it now  however it's easy to see why that never  happened the filmmakers seemed to have  read stanley and steve ditko's classic  comics and then decided to make them as  boring as possible instead of going to a  distant himalayan sanctuary to learn the  mystic arts steven strange meets up with  some old guy who lives in new york and  rather than being the daughter of an  extra dimensional warlord his love  interest clea is just a regular old  college student even if doctor strange  had gone to series it's hard to imagine  anyone would still watch it today  everyone in this movie seems to be in a  contest to see who can have the worst  hair while strange himself wears more  gold chains than mr t unsurprisingly the  disco soundtrack hasn't aged any better  and neither have the so-called magic  effects one scene in which strange  travels to the astral plane looks like  it should have been projected on the  wall of a hippie nightclub a decade  earlier overall only one thing in the  doctor strange pilot has actually aged  well an appearance by future arrested  development icon jessica walter as the  evil morgan lafaye  good for her  adapting a marvel comic book seems like  a can't miss proposition you've got the  characters all laid out for you a  built-in audience and decades worth of  stories ripe for adaptation but the  producers of many of these movies seem  to think what fans really love about the  comics is the names because that's about  all that made it into 1979's captain  america and its follow-up death too soon  the hero is still a military vet named  steve rogers but that's where the  resemblance to the comic books comes to  an end instead of doggedly trying to  enlist to fight the fascists rogers just  wants to drive around and discover  himself and while it's never easy for a  flesh and blood actor to pull off cap's  over-the-top get-up it's hard to imagine  a faithful adaptation looking any worse  than the ridiculous outfit steve dons  for the end of the first movie and the  rest of the second no wonder they kept  it off screen so long it's also kind of  hard to get viewers excited when the  cast all sound so damn bored including  red brown as cap himself put it this way  it's not exactly the winter soldier  in 1990 the head honcho was over and  marvel pulled up their socks wiped away  their tears and gave captain america  another go and  it was terrible while the 70s tv movie  had drastically altered cap's suit this  time the project suffered from the  opposite problem director albert pion  later said i petitioned them over and  over to let me adjust the captain  america costume but they were adamant it  had to be the one in the comic what  looks good on the page doesn't look good  on the screen however and certainly not  on a budget as a result poor cap was  saddled with this wrinkly rubbery mess  bizarrely inside that suit is matt  salinger son of the famously reclusive  catcher in the rye author jd salinger  the script doesn't do him any favors  either putting him in the role of a  superhero who never actually seems to  win a fight he even loses during his  first time out in world war ii when his  enemy the red skull straps him to a  missile aimed at the white house but hey  at least cap manages to steer it to a  harmless landing in the arctic ocean  which is mostly impressive and that it  makes absolutely no sense captain  america was the work of israeli american  super shlock master menachem golin so  nonsense is par for the course this was  one of the first movies he made after  the collapse of his canon film group  given that this was how he chose to  start his new venture a 21st century  films well you can guess how long that  lasted  the fantastic four may be one of the  most beloved teams in marvel comic books  and ushered in the marvel age but they  haven't had half as much luck in the  movies in the 90s producer baron  eichenger was about to lose his rights  to the characters and he needed a quick  cheap movie to keep them from expiring  so he went to a man who knew quick and  cheap b-movie maestro roger corman the  crew didn't realize they'd signed on for  a corporate write-off though and they  took it all dead seriously despite their  efforts this is an action movie so cheap  that it goes an hour without an action  sequence and when we finally got one  it's about as underwhelming as you could  possibly imagine the human torch appears  as a sub bowling alley cgi creation and  they couldn't even make mr fantastic's  gring temples look real and then it all  ends like this unsurprisingly the movie  never got an official release but the  jokes on eischinger as he still lost all  the rights but hey it could have been  worse  it's fantastic  say that again  before samuel l jackson put on nick  fury's eye patch the role belonged to  baywatch beefcake david hasselhoff in  fact hasselhoff claims stan lee himself  called him the ultimate nick fury though  the fans apparently disagreed since his  feature-length pilot never got a full  series this makes nick fury agent of  shield a deeply strange viewing  experience it opens with armed goons  stealing the frozen body of hydra  founder baron von strucker but he's not  actually thought out until the very end  of the movie instead the plot focuses on  strucker's daughter who plans to  overthrow the world's governments with  the so-called death said virus this  forces shield to recruit their former  director who they find wailing away at a  concrete wall in a mine shaft for  reasons we may never understand there  are also some hilarious special effects  on show here including a helicarrier  that they somehow managed to make even  more comic bookie than the comic book  supported on what looked like table legs  connected to skyscraper-sized  jet engines and then there's this robot  dude who inexplicably lights up his eyes  and wide open mouth as a kind of living  movie projector to deliver strucker's  message so what did the later fury think  of all this speaking about his  preparations for the role in an  interview jackson said i watched david  hasselhoff and decided i was not going  to do any of that  the first two issues of marvel are a  broad superhero parody focused on cal  aol a visitor from the future who tries  to become a superhero but with issue  number three the story really goes off  the deep end as what little plot the  reader is given disappears completely  and the characters go on a journey to  the dawn of time apparently this all  happens so the comics writer editor bill  gemez can put his cranked up theories  about the origins of life in the mouth  of god himself as if that wasn't  self-important enough number six has cal  aol recapped the series in the form of a  sales pitch to an unseen editor who  agrees that jamaz's crackpot ideas could  bring about world peace sadly they'll  never sell because the sheeple just want  to read about superheroes beating each  other up and then like the world's worst  party guest marvel just straight up  refuses to leave this six-issue  mini-series goes on to a seventh issue  with submission guidelines for yet  another forgotten failure jamaa's  short-lived revival of epic comics how  do you sell a book like this as it turns  out the covers didn't even try instead  featuring allegedly sexy art by greg  horn of a woman who never appears in the  book getting more and more undressed as  the sales dropped lower  the writing was on the wall for marvel  imprint epic comics when it launched  with trouble the idea was sound american  comics had largely ignored the female  half of their audience since the romance  genre went out in the 80s so why not  revive it for a new generation but the  execution was doomed from day one to  appeal to teen girls editor bill jamas  tapped kick-ass writer mark miller whose  primary audience was the kind of edgy  teen boys that teen girls normally avoid  predictably he went as over the top with  the sex as he normally does with  violence leeringly chronicling the over  the top erotic lives of teenagers named  may ben mary and richard if those names  sound familiar well there's the other  problem the series was heavily hinted to  be a spider-man prequel which means  marvel expected readers to get invested  in the love life of spidey's aunt may  worse the series introduced a major  rewrite of the superheroes secret  origins that writers and fans have  wisely ignored ever since may cheats on  ben with a whole bunch of people  including richard and he and mary end up  raising little baby peter as their own  that's right spider-man's aunt is really  his mother this soap opera twist didn't  help the series much and it flopped so  hard it took almost a decade before  marvel would reprint it  the ultimate universe was the farthest  thing from a forgotten failure but 10  years into its existence this new line  of updated heroes wasn't so new and up  to date anymore the ultimate line needed  a shakeup and it got one in jeff loeb  and david finch's 2009 ultimatum  miniseries but instead of opening up new  possibilities the series closed off  existing ones killing off so many  characters that there were hardly any  left to write about ultimatum only  begins to make sense in light of the  then contemporary torture fad in horror  movies because it's kind of like a super  powered song the plot follows magneto as  he decides to wipe out humanity for good  but this is basically just a thin excuse  to kill off character after character  some of their exits are insultingly  offhanded others are so gory they're  like a parody of adult comics the wasp  gets eaten alive dr strange appears for  all of two pages to fight a bad guy who  has nothing to do with the main plot  just so we can see the sorcerer  supreme's head turn purple and explode  even when the story seems to be over  loeb adds an epilogue with little  purpose but to add cyclops and dr doom's  deaths onto the pile fans weren't  impressed of course  and ultimatum ultimately lost tens of  thousands of readers by the end of its  five issue run  not every failure is deserved however  and it's a tragic fact that many great  comic book characters and stories have  been consigned to the footnotes of  history plenty of marvel series have  come and gone over the years without  ever selling enough to sustain  themselves no matter how cleverly  written or beautifully drawn they were  and that was the fate of thor the mighty  avenger the series brought in the iconic  writer roger language to create new  adventures for thor without all the  baggage of his decades of continuity he  came back with an origin story focusing  on thor's struggle to return to asgard  and his romance with jane foster chris  samny took on art duties creating  expressive characters and inventive  designs for new versions of classic  marvel characters but even the hype of  marvel's first thor movie wasn't enough  to keep the book afloat it ended after  less than a year and the final issue  suggests the creative team had little  notice since it concludes without  answering the story's most pressing  questions tragically roger langrage  hasn't worked for marvel since  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  marvel entertainment are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one

